== STAGE 3  DRIVE MOTORS (HA-14/15/17/111) ==========================================
CARTRIDGE is READ FROM EACH DEVICE -- a measurement, not our guess.
HA-15's invented stand-in was GREEN; whatever prints below is what is
actually fitted. Nothing here writes to a motor: this build never
constructs the adapter that would.

-- HYPOTHESISED LEFT --
  p15 GRN 200 raw=   500.0d canon=  8.727r 30.0C   27mA
  p16 GRN 200 raw=  -340.0d canon= -5.934r 30.0C    0mA
  p17 GRN 200 raw=   499.0d canon=  8.709r 30.0C    0mA
  p18 GRN 200 raw=  -503.0d canon= -8.779r 30.0C    0mA
-- HYPOTHESISED RIGHT --
  p11 GRN 200 raw=   418.0d canon=  7.295r 30.0C    0mA
  p12 GRN 200 raw=  -423.0d canon= -7.383r 30.0C    0mA
  p13 GRN 200 raw=   351.0d canon=  6.126r 30.0C    0mA
  p14 GRN 200 raw=  -426.0d canon= -7.435r 30.0C   30mA

TWO SEPARATE QUESTIONS. Do them in this order -- 'spin a wheel
forward' is AMBIGUOUS on its own (forward for the wheel, or for
the robot? seen from which side?), and a wrong reading here
mirrors every turn the library will ever command.

STEP 1 - WHICH WAY IS POSITIVE
  a. Decide which end of the robot is its FRONT, and PHOTOGRAPH
     it. The library's frame is +X forward, so this choice is
     part of the measurement -- not an obvious fact.
  b. Note every port's position above, then PUSH THE WHOLE ROBOT
     FORWARD a foot or so, along the floor, front end leading.
  c. Re-run this test. Every drive port moved. For EACH one write
     down whether it went UP or DOWN.
  Pushing the ROBOT removes the ambiguity: there is only one
  forward for a robot, and no left/right or near/far side to it.

STEP 2 - WHICH PORT IS WHICH WHEEL
  a. Lift the robot so the wheels are off the ground.
  b. Spin ONE wheel. Any direction -- it does not matter here.
  c. Re-run. Exactly one port's number changed: that port drives
     that wheel. Repeat per wheel.

No motor is ever powered. Both steps are safe with the robot on a
bench, and re-running this test is free.

